{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787127487,"nonce":"+hkSy+aBY/nqh0wpVmrQ8llmc2U=","message":"⚠️ **Cross-reference from a concurrent AFK session, and an apology for the noise.** I filed `728f70e` on the same subject at 13:06 without re-listing the queue; this ticket is 16 minutes older and is the primary. I have closed mine. Three things mine carried that this one does not are folded in below so nothing is lost when it goes.\n\n**⭐ And one correction that runs the other way: this ticket is RIGHT where mine was WRONG.** My Done-when said `ReasonUnackedReminder` should leave the enum, the two CHECKs and `MaxPolicyReasons` \"with a forward migration in `00060`'s shape\". That is the DELETE path, and the ⚠️ clause here is the reason it is wrong — `notifications.reason` holds up to thirteen months of rows, the `00051`/`00054` bargain is retire-and-refuse-at-the-writer, and `a51a763` clause 5 asked for exactly that for `storm` while the branch did the opposite. Anyone working from my ticket would have spent that concession a second time. **Follow this ticket's clause, not mine.**\n\n**1 · There is a compile-time SCOPE-BOUNDARY gate whose entire purpose is to make this concept hard to move, and it is not in the file list.** `test/scope/scalar_reminder_test.go` is AC-52 (SPEC §P-19c): four AST instruments proving `unacked_reminder_after_s` is a scalar `*int` in every layer. Its own header states the rule — *\"ONE STAGE, FOREVER\"* — and explains that a grep deliberately cannot do its job, because `*int`, `[]int`, `[]Stage` and `map[int][]string` all match the same line. **It should be DELETED, not weakened**: a gate over a field that no longer exists is worse than no gate, because it goes green for the wrong reason.\n\n**2 · This reverses BINDING boundary text, not just code, and that part has no template.** `docs/design/SCOPE-BOUNDARY.md:185` is the door decision: `escalation` was **RESHAPED, not cut** — the reason renamed to `unacked_reminder`, the worker `escalation.check` → `notify.unacked_reminder`, `escalate_after_s` → `unacked_reminder_after_s`, individual `\u003c@U…\u003e` mentions dropped from `mention_on_reminder` (`:186`), and a binding clause added to §G.9 fixing oto at one stage forever. `:266` and `:384` restate it as the ruling. Removing the mechanism means those lines describe something that no longer exists. They get a recorded supersession — not a silent edit — or `SCOPE-BOUNDARY.md` starts lying the way `00011:197` did in `e5c060b`. The enum narrowing has a good template in `00060`; **this does not**, and it is the part that needs a person.\n\n**3 · Two sub-questions are genuinely undecided, and either answer makes the rest mechanical.**\n   - **Does `mention_on_reminder` go too?** Three of the four settings keys are the mention subsystem (`internal/identity/domain/settings.go:53,55,59`), so this is most of the settings surface. If the only thing that mentions is the reminder, they go together. If a mention is a property of any Slack delivery, they stay and only the trigger goes. Not determined — nothing was run.\n   - **What happens to the §G.9 one-stage-forever clause and the `escalation` banned word?** With no stage at all, does the clause become \"there are no reminder stages and never will be\", or retire with the mechanism? The banned word almost certainly stays — it was banned for reasons unrelated to whether oto reminds — but that should be said, not assumed.\n\n**On the file count:** you have 71 excluding `docs-site` and `node_modules`; I measured 95 including them. Same surface, different exclusions.\n\n**Nothing in this ticket's surface was touched by my session.** My two commits (`ec9dee4`, `4784fd0`) are the `7570090` stage-2 revert and the `e5c060b` `frozen` removal; neither goes near `reminders.go`, the settings keys, the Helm values, or the mention path — verified by `git show --stat`. They are unpushed on local `main`.",